course: everyday
lesson: 18
title: Remembering details
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Noticing details means looking carefully and putting what you see into words, which helps you remember it.
explain: Useful details about a person: their height compared with someone you know, their hair, their clothes and colours, and anything special such as glasses or a cap with a logo.
explain: Useful details about a vehicle: its colour, its type (car, van, truck), the letters and numbers on its number plate (licence plate), and which way it went.
explain: Memory fades and gets mixed up quickly, so tell an adult or write down what you saw as soon as you can.
explain: Example: a boy in Manchester sees a bike being stolen and says to himself, «tall man, green hoodie, white trainers, rode off towards the park» — then tells a teacher straight away.
explain: A boy in New Jersey saw a car hit a bike and drive away, but he could not remember the colour or the plate; the police had little to go on.
explain: A girl in Edinburgh got lost but knew her full name, her mum's name and phone number by heart; a police officer called her mum and she was home within an hour.
fact: Putting what you see into words helps you remember it.
fact: Clothes, colours and special marks are easy details to notice about a person.
fact: Comparing someone's height with a person you know, like your dad, is easier than guessing a number.
fact: Every car has a number plate, also called a licence plate, with letters and numbers.
fact: The colour and type of a vehicle are useful details for the police.
fact: The direction someone went is an important detail.
fact: Memories fade quickly, so it helps to tell someone or write them down soon.
fact: Memories can change when people talk about them, so tell what you saw yourself, not what others said.
fact: If you are not sure about a detail, it is honest and helpful to say «I'm not sure».
fact: Memory games, like looking at objects on a tray and then naming them, train your attention.
fact: Never follow a person or a car to get details; staying safe comes first.
fact: Children who know their full name, address and a parent's phone number by heart are found and helped much faster.
fact: Small details like a car's colour, a number plate or what someone wore help police a lot.
exercise: Which detail is most useful for finding a car? | Its number plate | How shiny it is | Whether it has a radio | How many seats it has
exercise: What helps you remember what you saw? | Saying it to yourself in words | Trying not to think about it | Waiting a week before telling | Closing your eyes while it happens
exercise: Why should you tell an adult what you saw quickly? | Memories fade and get mixed up over time | Adults stop listening to children after one whole day | Details get clearer as time passes | The police only take reports at noon
exercise: What is the easiest way to describe a person's height? | Compare it with someone you know | Guess the exact height in centimetres | Count how many steps they take | Say whether they seemed friendly
exercise: You saw a van drive away fast after a crash. Which details are good to tell? | Its colour, type and which way it went | The song playing loudly on its radio as it drove off | How clean its tyres looked | What the driver ate that day
exercise: You are not sure whether a man's coat was blue or black. What should you say? | That you are not sure | That it was definitely blue | That it was definitely black | Nothing at all about the coat
exercise: Why should you not follow a suspicious car to read its plate? | Your safety comes first | It is against the rules of the road | The plate is easier to read later | Cars move too slowly to follow
exercise: Which details about a person are easiest to notice and remember? | Clothes, colours and special marks | Their shoe size and their exact age in years | Their name and their home town | What they had for breakfast
exercise: Your friend tells you what he thinks he saw. Why should you tell the police your own memory? | Hearing others can change your memory | His memory is always wrong, so yours is better | Police want only the shortest story | Two stories cannot both be told
exercise: What does a memory tray game train? | Your attention to details | Your running speed | Your singing voice and how loud it is | Your sense of taste
exercise: What is a licence plate? | A plate on a vehicle with letters and numbers | A paper that lets a child ride a bike on the road | The sticker that shows a car's colour | A sign that tells drivers the speed limit
exercise: A girl in Ohio sees a loose dog with a red collar and a white patch on its back. Why remember these details? | They help the owner know it is their dog | They tell you what the dog is called | They show that the dog is dangerous | They prove that the dog lives nearby
exercise: Which detail is least useful in describing a person? | That they had two arms | That they wore a yellow cap | That they had a red beard | That they wore round glasses
exercise: What is the best way to remember a car's number plate? | Say it to yourself or write it down at once | Look at it once and simply trust your memory later | Remember only the colour of the plate | Try hard to recall it the next day
test: Jake saw a man take a bag from a café table. He wants to help. What is best? | Tell an adult right away what he saw | Run after the man to see his face | Wait until tomorrow to think it over | Keep it to himself in case he is wrong
test: Which description would help the police most? | Short woman, red coat, white bag, went towards the station | A person who looked a bit odd to me, I didn't like the look of them | Someone who was near the shop at some point | A woman, I think, wearing some clothes
test: Why can talking with friends about what you all saw be a problem? | Memories can change to match what others say | Friends always make up stories to sound more exciting | It makes everyone's memory perfect | The police do not allow it
test: Which is true about memory? | It fades and can change over time | It stays exactly the same for ever | It gets sharper every day | It records everything like a camera
test: A car hits a fence and drives off. What is most useful to remember? | Colour, type of car and part of the plate | What the weather was like the day before the crash | What the fence was made of | How many birds were nearby
test: You are asked what shoes a person wore, but you did not see them. What should you say? | I did not see their shoes | They wore black shoes, probably | Whatever shoes seem most likely | Their shoes were the same as mine
test: A girl in Brisbane is asked how tall a man was. Which answer is most useful? | About as tall as my dad | Very tall, I think, quite big | Medium, maybe | Not that short
test: Why does saying «green hoodie, white shoes» to yourself help? | Words make the picture easier to remember | Saying it out loud makes the person stop and wait | It changes what the person wears | It warns everybody nearby
test: What should you never do to get details? | Follow a stranger or a car | Notice what someone is wearing | Say what you see in your head | Tell a trusted adult quickly
test: saw a car hit a fence n drive off, what do i tell police | Colour, type, plate and which way it went | Only how loud it was when it crashed | What other people said | Nothing, it's not my business
test: shall i run after the car to get the plate? | No, stay safe, never follow | Yes, run as fast as you can | Yes, if you have a phone | Yes, follow on your bike
test: police asked how tall he was, dunno | Compare him to someone you know, like dad | Make up a number | Say what your friend said | Say he was very tall just to be safe, police like that
test: not sure if his jacket was blue or black | Say you're not sure | Pick blue to sound sure | Say what the others said | Say both, to be safe
test: lost at the station, police asks mum's number and i forgot it | Learn it by heart now, it gets you home much faster | It does not matter, they will just guess it from your name | Numbers are only for grown-ups | Just give them any number
test: saw a car drive off after hitting a dog, what should i remember | The colour, the type and the plate if you can | Only how loud it was | Nothing, it is not your job | Just which way the dog ran
test: Mason says u don't need ur address if u got a phone. true? | No, phones die or get lost, so know it by heart | Yes, phones always work | Yes, the phone knows where you live, so it can tell people | Yes, unless you move house
test: everyone's saying the robber at the corner shop was 7 foot tall with a scar, my mate's mum saw him | Rumours change as they spread; tell only what you saw yourself, and the details may have grown | It must be exactly true because a mum saw it | It proves robbers are always very tall | It is fine to tell police what the gossip says as your own
test: kid said he followed the car that hit the cat and got the number plate, he's a hero | Getting the plate is useful, but following a car is unsafe; safety comes first | He did the right thing, following cars is always smart if you want to catch them | He should have chased it on his bike | The plate only matters if you follow the car home
test: they're saying Aria's brother was the one who smashed the bus shelter, someone said they saw him | Do not spread it; it may be untrue and it could hurt Aria and her family | Spread it, because someone saw him do it, so it must be true | Spread it, so the police hear about it | Spread it, and add what he was wearing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of conversation upon a memory of an event? | Memories may be altered through discussion, wherefore one should report what one personally observed | Memories become wholly fixed once they have been discussed | Discussion invariably renders a memory more accurate | Memories are unaffected by any conversation
exam: It being given that a witness is uncertain of a particular detail, select the response that is most appropriate. | To state honestly that he is not sure | To supply the most probable detail as though it were certain | To repeat the detail as reported by others | To decline to give any account whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an account of an event ought to be given or written down promptly. | Memories fade with rapidity | Memories become more detailed with the passage of time | The police accept accounts only on the same day | Written accounts are invariably true
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning the gathering of details about a suspicious vehicle. | The child must not follow the person or vehicle, safety taking precedence over details | The child should follow the vehicle so as to read its number plate | The child should approach the driver to request his name | The child should photograph the driver at close range
